    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Multicollinearity\"\nsubtitle: \"When predictors overlap\"\nauthor: \"Prof. Eric Friedlander\"\nfooter: \"[🔗 MATH 2025 - Fall 2026 - Schedule](https://ericfriedlander.github.io/math2025-fa26/)\"\nlogo: \"../images/logo.png\"\nformat:\n  revealjs:\n    theme: slides.scss\n    multiplex: false\n    transition: fade\n    slide-number: false\n    incremental: false\n    chalkboard: true\n    scrollable: true\nhtml-math-method:\n  method: mathjax\n  url: \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@3/es5/tex-mml-chtml.js\"\nexecute:\n  freeze: auto\n  echo: true\n  cache: false\nknitr:\n  opts_chunk:\n    R.options:\n    width: 200\nbibliography: references.bib\n---\n\n\n\n## Topics\n\n::: nonincremental\n-   What multicollinearity is, and why it is a problem\n-   Detecting it: plots, correlations, and the variance inflation factor (VIF)\n-   What to do about it\n:::\n\n## Data: `rail_trail` {.smaller}\n\nDaily `volume` of users on a rail trail in Massachusetts over 90 days in 2005 (Pioneer Valley Planning Commission), with weather and calendar variables.\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\nRows: 90\nColumns: 7\n$ volume     <dbl> 501, 419, 397, 385, 200, 375, 417, 629, 533, 547, 432, 418,…\n$ hightemp   <dbl> 83, 73, 74, 95, 44, 69, 66, 66, 80, 79, 78, 65, 41, 59, 50,…\n$ avgtemp    <dbl> 66.5, 61.0, 63.0, 78.0, 48.0, 61.5, 52.5, 52.0, 67.5, 62.0,…\n$ season     <chr> \"Summer\", \"Summer\", \"Spring\", \"Summer\", \"Spring\", \"Spring\",…\n$ cloudcover <dbl> 7.6, 6.3, 7.5, 2.6, 10.0, 6.6, 2.4, 0.0, 3.8, 4.1, 8.5, 7.2…\n$ precip     <dbl> 0.00, 0.29, 0.32, 0.00, 0.14, 0.02, 0.00, 0.00, 0.00, 0.00,…\n$ day_type   <chr> \"Weekday\", \"Weekday\", \"Weekday\", \"Weekend\", \"Weekday\", \"Wee…\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n# A puzzle\n\n## One predictor {.smaller}\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nfit_high <- lm(volume ~ hightemp, data = rail_trail)\ntidy(fit_high) |> kable(digits = 2)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term        | estimate| std.error| statistic| p.value|\n|:-----------|--------:|---------:|---------:|-------:|\n|(Intercept) |   -17.08|     59.40|     -0.29|    0.77|\n|hightemp    |     5.70|      0.85|      6.72|    0.00|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nEach additional degree of high temperature is associated with about **5.7** more riders, and the standard error is **0.85**.\n\n## Add the average temperature {.smaller}\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nfit_both <- lm(volume ~ hightemp + avgtemp, data = rail_trail)\ntidy(fit_both) |> kable(digits = 2)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|term        | estimate| std.error| statistic| p.value|\n|:-----------|--------:|---------:|---------:|-------:|\n|(Intercept) |     1.67|     56.42|      0.03|    0.98|\n|hightemp    |    12.06|      2.04|      5.91|    0.00|\n|avgtemp     |    -7.94|      2.35|     -3.39|    0.00|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: question\nWhat changed for `hightemp`? What does the coefficient on `avgtemp` seem to say: do warmer average days mean **fewer** riders?\n:::\n\n. . .\n\n-   The `hightemp` estimate more than **doubled**, and its standard error went from 0.85 to 2.04\n-   `avgtemp` has a **negative** coefficient, even though warmer days clearly bring more riders\n\n## What is going on?\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](23-multicollinearity_files/figure-revealjs/unnamed-chunk-4-1.png){fig-align='center' width=80%}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n`hightemp` and `avgtemp` carry nearly the same information. This is **multicollinearity**.\n\n# What is multicollinearity?\n\n## Definition\n\n**Multicollinearity** is when one or more predictors are strongly related to some combination of the other predictors.\n\n. . .\n\n**Test of intuition:** if you could fit a good linear model with one predictor as the *response* and the other predictors as the *explanatory variables*, then your predictors are exhibiting multicollinearity.\n\n## The extreme case {.smaller}\n\nSuppose the truth is $y = 3 + 4x$, and we fit a model with $x$ and $z = x/10$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{y}&= \\hat{\\beta}_0 + \\hat{\\beta}_1x  + \\hat{\\beta}_2z\\\\\n&= \\hat{\\beta}_0 + \\hat{\\beta}_1x  + \\hat{\\beta}_2\\frac{x}{10}\\\\\n&= \\hat{\\beta}_0 + \\bigg(\\hat{\\beta}_1 + \\frac{\\hat{\\beta}_2}{10}\\bigg)x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n. . .\n\n-   Any $\\hat\\beta_1$ and $\\hat\\beta_2$ with $\\hat{\\beta}_1 + \\frac{\\hat{\\beta}_2}{10} = 4$ give **exactly the same predictions**\n-   So the data cannot tell us which pair is \"best\": the model is **unidentifiable**, and R will give `NA` for one coefficient\n-   This is also why a categorical variable needs a reference level: the indicators for all levels always add up to 1, which is perfectly collinear with the intercept\n\n::: question\nReal data are almost never perfectly collinear. What do you think happens when predictors are *nearly* collinear?\n:::\n\n## Highly correlated predictors: a wobbly plane {.smaller}\n\n`X1` and `X2` are highly correlated ($r = 0.95$). The truth: `Y` depends on `X2` only (true `X1` slope = 0).\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div id=\"htmlwidget-730f0e8140ffc2f7c865\" style=\"width:80%;height:450px;\" class=\"plotly html-widget\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-730f0e8140ffc2f7c865\">{\"x\":{\"visdat\":{\"25fe9e75db2577\":[\"function () \",\"plotlyVisDat\"]},\"cur_data\":\"25fe9e75db2577\",\"attrs\":{\"25fe9e75db2577\":{\"alpha_stroke\":1,\"sizes\":[10,100],\"spans\":[1,20],\"x\":[-0.81031547189302355,0.10332756866208691,-1.0462268133293786,1.6953993114427977,0.26783628206948396,-1.0291288179478124,0.44594292232327293,0.72890783739561826,0.54558833497803594,-0.44821174733770647,1.6012268277809114,0.33588377285499404,-0.80443589117862691,-2.6015697694257427,1.1649297176248625,-0.15446586779152416,-0.12204857259909603,0.960687643422