{\"on\":\"plotly_click\",\"persistent\":false,\"dynamic\":false,\"selectize\":false,\"opacityDim\":0.20000000000000001,\"selected\":{\"opacity\":1},\"debounce\":0},\"shinyEvents\":[\"plotly_hover\",\"plotly_click\",\"plotly_selected\",\"plotly_relayout\",\"plotly_brushed\",\"plotly_brushing\",\"plotly_clickannotation\",\"plotly_doubleclick\",\"plotly_deselect\",\"plotly_afterplot\",\"plotly_sunburstclick\"],\"base_url\":\"https://plot.ly\"},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":{\"render\":[{\"code\":\"\\n      function(el, x, d) {\\n        var n = d.x1.length, L = d.lim;\\n        function gauss() { var u = 1 - Math.random(), v = Math.random();\\n                           return Math.sqrt(-2 * Math.log(u)) * Math.cos(2 * Math.PI * v); }\\n        // least squares for y ~ 1 + x1 + x2 via the normal equations (3 x 3 Gaussian elimination)\\n        function fit(y) {\\n          var A = [[0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0]], c = [0,0,0], i, j, k, row;\\n          for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n            row = [1, d.x1[i], d.x2[i]];\\n            for (j = 0; j < 3; j++) { c[j] += row[j] * y[i];\\n                                      for (k = 0; k < 3; k++) A[j][k] += row[j] * row[k]; }\\n          }\\n          for (j = 0; j < 3; j++) {\\n            for (i = j + 1; i < 3; i++) { var f = A[i][j] / A[j][j];\\n              for (k = j; k < 3; k++) A[i][k] -= f * A[j][k]; c[i] -= f * c[j]; }\\n          }\\n          var b = [0,0,0];\\n          for (j = 2; j >= 0; j--) { var s = c[j];\\n            for (k = j + 1; k < 3; k++) s -= A[j][k] * b[k]; b[j] = s / A[j][j]; }\\n          return b;\\n        }\\n        el.on('plotly_buttonclicked', function() {\\n          var y = [], i;\\n          for (i = 0; i < n; i++) y.push(d.beta[0] + d.beta[1] * d.x1[i] + d.beta[2] * d.x2[i] + d.sigma * gauss());\\n          var b = fit(y), e = [-L, L];\\n          var z = e.map(function(x2) { return e.map(function(x1) { return b[0] + b[1] * x1 + b[2] * x2; }); });\\n          Plotly.restyle(el, {z: [y]}, [0]);\\n          Plotly.restyle(el, {z: [z]}, [1]);\\n          Plotly.relayout(el, {'annotations[0].text': 'Fitted slopes: b1 = ' + b[1].toFixed(2) +\\n            ', b2 = ' + b[2].toFixed(2) + '   (truth: b1 = ' + d.beta[1] + ', b2 = ' + d.beta[2] + ')'});\\n        });\\n      }\",\"data\":{\"x1\":[-0.81031547189302355,0.10332756866208691,-1.0462268133293786,1.6953993114427977,0.26783628206948396,-1.0291288179478124,0.44594292232327293,0.72890783739561826,0.54558833497803594,-0.44821174733770647,1.6012268277809114,0.33588377285499404,-0.80443589117862691,-2.6015697694257427,1.1649297176248625,-0.15446586779152416,-0.12204857259909603,0.96068764342291035,0.82240008403815534,0.56602438316620951,0.932651369328382,0.77831939273830353,-0.01969307009572693,-2.3474177698573477,0.59526244733372047,-0.16709195007295499,-0.27949803605334683,-1.7625315484865236,-0.64305566022997063,0.36757359975648768,1.4285558964061382,-0.21971484880407391,0.33343457281817368,-0.16447123796472268,-1.6568629863988311,-0.5718276888255186,-0.54847663418991199,-0.1706836673442238,1.1368407663898976,0.75693531929562119],\"x2\":[-0.12497744177064088,-0.43899076566632683,0.88666691226257088,0.084526306280947636,-0.96095047227831742,-0.67593621981888596,0.16597573541747218,0.54753277188333915,-0.38678168038974409,0.95001232908470346,-0.069224885355866017,-0.89184640928855308,0.43476440869088867,-0.77333210405768815,1.1821298607036705,2.1006692483742802,-0.51220334666352174,-1.5186922867137873,0.30485899294582586,-0.41680048786040458,2.311777683811207,-0.36052883892157706,0.63660489997085579,0.45110613260766252,-1.0986537322140875,0.11544328213789912,-2.0705804459736035,1.90849368409557,0.18823156058429852,2.19088154020185,0.057386262469098157,-0.86950733371306776,0.46565809850583029,-1.1312807726536065,-1.1339901591318353,0.32479377865303327,-0.49607402923655014,-0.091991914145754014,-0.31901945684234373,-0.96615070598484287],\"beta\":[2,0,1],\"sigma\":1.5,\"lim\":2.5}}]}}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: question\nClick **New noise** several times. Compare with the last slide: why does the plane wobble so much less?\n:::\n\n. . .\n\nWhen the predictors move together, the data cover only a thin strip of the $(X_1, X_2)$ floor. The plane is **pinned down along the strip but free to tilt** across it, so the individual slopes are poorly determined, even though predictions *on the strip* stay good.\n\n## Nearly collinear: the coefficients wobble {.smaller}\n\nHere is the same idea over many samples. Each simulated study below has the **same true model**, $y = 2 + 0 \\cdot x_1 + 1 \\cdot x_2 + \\text{noise}$, but a different correlation between $x_1$ and $x_2$. We fit 200 samples of $n = 40$ for each.\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](23-multicollinearity_files/figure-revealjs/unnamed-chunk-7-1.png){fig-align='center' width=100%}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n. . .\n\nThe estimates are still centered at the truth, but the more correlated the predictors, the **more they vary from sample to sample**. That is exactly a larger standard error.\n\n# Why it matters\n\n## Consequences\n\n-   **Standard errors inflate**: wide intervals, small $t$-statistics, large p-values, even for variables that are truly related to $y$\n-   **Estimates are unstable**: a small change in the data can swing a coefficient a lot, even flip its sign\n-   **Interpretation breaks down**: \"holding `avgtemp` constant, a 1 degree increase in `hightemp`...\" describes a day that basically does not exist\n\n. . .\n\nWhat multicollinearity does **not** hurt: the model's **predictions** and $R^2$. If you only care about predicting $y$, it is mostly harmless. It is a problem when you want to **interpret or test individual coefficients**, which is what most of our projects do.\n\n# Detecting multicollinearity\n\n## In the exploratory analysis\n\n-   Look at the **correlations** among the predictors. Values near $\\pm 1$ (rule of thumb: above $|0.8|$ or $|0.9|$) are a warning sign.\n-   Look at **scatterplots** of pairs of quantitative predictors. A tight linear band is a warning sign.\n-   For categorical predictors, look at **boxplots** or **tables** of one predictor against another.\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nrail_trail |>\n  select(hightemp, avgtemp, cloudcover, precip) |>\n  cor() |>\n  round(2)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n           hightemp avgtemp cloudcover precip\nhightemp       1.00    0.92      -0.10   0.13\navgtemp        0.92    1.00       0.14   0.27\ncloudcover    -0.10    0.14       1.00   0.37\nprecip         0.13    0.27       0.37   1.00\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Pairwise checks can miss it\n\nA predictor can be nearly predictable from a **combination** of several others, even if no single pair is highly correlated. We need a number that captures \"how well do all the other predictors predict this one?\"\n\n## Variance inflation factor (VIF) {.smaller}\n\nFor predictor $x_j$, regress it on **all the other predictors**, and let $R^2_j$ be the proportion of the variation in $x_j$ that they explain. Then\n\n$$VIF_j = \\frac{1}{1 - R^2_j}$$\n\n. . .\n\n-   $R_j^2 = 0$ (no overlap) $\\Rightarrow$ $VIF = 1$\n-   $R_j^2 = 0.9$ $\\Rightarrow$ $VIF = 10$\n-   Interpretation: the variance of $\\hat\\beta_j$ is inflated by a factor of $VIF_j$ (its standard error by $\\sqrt{VIF_j}$)\n-   Rules of thumb: $VIF > 5$ deserves a look, $VIF > 10$ is concerning\n\n## Computing VIF {.smaller}\n\nThe `vif()` function from the `car` package computes the VIF of every predictor in a model at once:\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlibrary(car)\nvif(fit_both)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\nhightemp  avgtemp \n6.482768 6.482768 \n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n. . .\n\nSo the standard error of $\\hat\\beta_{hightemp}$ is inflated by roughly $\\sqrt{6.5} \\approx 2.5$, which is about what we saw: 0.85 $\\to$ 2.04.\n\n::: question\nWhy are the VIFs of `hightemp` and `avgtemp` identical here?\n:::\n\n. . .\n\nWith only two predictors, both VIFs are $1/(1 - r^2)$, where $r$ is the correlation between them. With more predictors, each has its own.\n\n## VIFs for a bigger model {.smaller}\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nfit_big <- lm(volume ~ hightemp + avgtemp + season + cloudcover + precip + day_type,\n              data = rail_trail)\nvif(fit_big)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n                GVIF Df GVIF^(1/(2*Df))\nhightemp   10.259978  1        3.203120\navgtemp    13.086175  1        3.617482\nseason      3.207204  2        1.338233\ncloudcover  1.587485  1        1.259954\nprecip      1.295352  1        1.138135\nday_type    1.125741  1        1.061009\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n. . .\n\n::: question\nWhich predictors are worth worrying about? Which are not? What would you do?\n:::\n\n. . .\n\n-   `hightemp` and `avgtemp` are the correlated pair\n-   `season` may be somewhat inflated too, because season is tied to temperature\n-   `cloudcover`, `precip`, and `day_type` are fine\n-   Because `season` and `day_type` are categorical, `vif()` reports a **generalized VIF**: see the next slide\n\n## Generalized VIF (GVIF) {.smaller}\n\nA categorical predictor with $k$ levels enters the model as $k - 1$ indicator variables. Those indicators are correlated with each other by construction, so judging each one's VIF separately is misleading (it depends on which level you chose as the reference). Instead, we ask about the **whole predictor at once**.\n\n-   **GVIF**: how much the uncertainty about *all* of a predictor's coefficients, taken together, is inflated by correlation with the other predictors. For a quantitative predictor (`Df = 1`) it is exactly the ordinary VIF.\n-   **`Df`**: the number of coefficients the predictor uses ($k - 1$ for a categorical predictor).\n-   **$GVIF^{1/(2 \\cdot Df)}$**: GVIF rescaled so that predictors with different `Df` can be compared. It lives on the **standard error scale**: it says how many times larger the standard errors are, roughly, than with uncorrelated predictors.\n\n. . .\n\n**How to interpret and decide**\n\n-   Compare $GVIF^{1/(2 \\cdot Df)}$ to $\\sqrt{5} \\approx 2.2$ (worth a look) and $\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.2$ (concerning). These are the usual VIF cutoffs of 5 and 10, put on the same scale.\n-   Below the cutoffs: no action needed, move on.\n-   Above them: first ask whether the inflation matters for your goal. If you only want **predictions**, it usually does not. If you want to **interpret or test** that predictor's effect, wide intervals and unstable coefficients are a real problem.\n-   If it matters, your options: drop or combine redundant predictors, collect more varied data, or keep the model and say honestly that the effects cannot be separated.\n-   Never drop a predictor just because of a VIF if the research question needs it.\n\n::: callout-note\nTwo caveats. (1) A model with an **interaction** or **squared term** will show large VIFs for the terms involved, because $x$ and $x^2$ (or $x$ and $x \\cdot z$) are naturally related. That is expected, and usually not a reason to drop them. (2) Do not apply the VIF cutoffs to dummy variables of a categorical predictor one at a time: that is why `vif()` gives one generalized VIF per term.\n:::\n\n# Categorical predictors and study design\n\n## Categorical predictors too\n\nMulticollinearity is not only about two numbers:\n\n-   A categorical predictor can be tied to another (e.g. an Islander's `village` and their `occupation`): check with a **two-way table** or a **boxplot**\n-   A categorical predictor can be tied to a quantitative one (e.g. `season` and `hightemp`: summer days are hot)\n\n. . .\n\n::: question\nIn your Islands study, if you **randomly assigned** the primary explanatory variable, should it be related to the demographic variables? What if your study is observational?\n:::\n\n. . .\n\nRandomization breaks the link between the treatment and everything else, so multicollinearity between the treatment and the other variables should be small. In an **observational** study they are often tangled together, which is exactly what makes it hard to separate their effects.\n\n# What to do\n\n## Options\n\n1.  **Drop** one of the redundant predictors and refit.\n    -   Example: keep `hightemp`, drop `avgtemp`.\n2.  **Combine** the redundant predictors into one.\n    -   Example: a single `temp_composite` that is the average of `avgtemp` and `hightemp`.\n3.  **Keep both, but don't interpret them individually.** If you care about the *pair*, test them together with an $F$-test (Lecture 20) instead of one at a time.\n\n. . .\n\n::: question\nWhich option would you pick if your research question is *\"how does temperature affect ridership?\"* Would your answer change if the question were *\"does the high temperature matter beyond the average temperature?\"*\n:::\n\n## Do **not** panic {.smaller}\n\n-   If your **primary variable** is not involved in the multicollinearity, you can leave it alone: the standard error you care about is not inflated\n-   Control variables included on purpose (confounders) can be highly related to your primary variable, and that is fine, as long as your primary variable's interval is still informative\n-   Always report what you found: \"VIFs were all below 3\" is a one-line sentence in a Methods section\n\n## Activity: HW 11, Part 2 {.smaller}\n\n::: appex\n📋 [HW 11 - Conditions and Multicollinearity](https://ericfriedlander.github.io/math2025-fa26/hw/hw-11.html)\n\nUse the model you checked on Monday. Work on **Part 2**: look at how your explanatory variables relate to each other, compare standard errors, compute VIFs, and decide whether multicollinearity is a problem for your project.\n:::\n\n::: callout-important\n**Use `vif()` from the `car` package** (`library(car)`, then `vif(your_model)`) for the VIF exercise, instead of the prewritten `glance()` code in the answer box. It gives you the VIF of every predictor in one step. If your model has a categorical predictor, read the generalized VIF column ($GVIF^{1/(2 \\cdot Df)}$) as described on the previous slides.\n:::\n\n\n::: {.cell layout-align=\"center\"}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<countdown-timer class=\"countdown\" id=\"timer_f862680b\" minutes=\"30\" seconds=\"0\" update-every=\"1\" tabindex=\"0\" style=\"right:0;bottom:0;\"></countdown-timer>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Recap\n\n-   **Multicollinearity**: a predictor is strongly related to a combination of the other predictors\n-   It inflates standard errors, makes estimates unstable, and makes individual coefficients hard to interpret. Predictions are not hurt.\n-   Detect it with correlations, plots, and $VIF_j = 1/(1 - R_j^2)$ (worry above 5 to 10)\n-   Fix it by dropping or combining predictors, or by testing the correlated predictors together and not interpreting them individually\n",
